Chapter: VecReprMix
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The two vector families have two runtime layouts and this chapter is the
 refusal that keeps them apart.

 A packed `Vector n T` is n eight-byte lanes with no header, so lane 0 is at
 offset 0. A `SizedVec n T` has a list's layout, the count at offset 0 and the
 elements from offset 8. The two are off by exactly one word, so a sized
 reader applied to a packed value returns lane 0 as the count: before the
 split, `vec-length (vec-splat 7)` compiled clean and answered 7, where the
 length of a two-lane vector is 2.

 BOTH ROWS COMPILED CLEAN BEFORE THE SPLIT AND THAT IS WHY THEY ARE THE ROWS.
 An arm that refuses at head as well proves nothing about this change, and the
 first draft of this chapter was one: it read `vec-extract (vec-singleton 7) 0`,
 which head already refuses because a `Vector 2` parameter does not take a
 `Vector 1` argument, so the whole file refused with or without the fix. The
 second row below takes its argument from `vec-cons`, whose derived length is
 2, so at head the arities agree and the packed reader silently reads a list's
 count word as lane 0.

Section: The Sized Reader On A Packed Value

  sized-reader-on-packed : Integer
  sized-reader-on-packed = vec-length (vec-splat 7)

Section: The Packed Reader On A Sized Value

  packed-reader-on-sized : Integer
  packed-reader-on-sized = vec-extract (vec-cons 1 (vec-singleton 2)) 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (show sized-reader-on-packed)
    print-line-uni (show packed-reader-on-sized)
  end
